description:
In the E2AP dissector the RANfunctionDefinition field is searched to see if a substring which corresponds to one of the E2SM models' names is somewhere in the beginning of the tvb:

```c
#.FN_BODY RANfunctionDefinition  VAL_PTR = &parameter_tvb
  tvbuff_t *parameter_tvb;
  %(DEFAULT_BODY)s
  /* Looking for shortName string near beginning of tvb */
  gboolean found = FALSE;
  for (int n=KPM_RANFUNCTIONS; n<MAX_RANFUNCTIONS; n++) {
    guint32 tvb_len = tvb_captured_length(parameter_tvb);
    guint name_len = (gint)strlen(g_ran_functioname_table[n].name);
    for (int m=0; (m<30) && ((m+name_len+1))<tvb_len; m++) {
      if (strcmp(tvb_get_ptr(parameter_tvb, m, name_len),
                 g_ran_functioname_table[n].name) == 0) {
        /* Call the set's dissector */
        g_ran_functioname_table[n].functions.ran_function_definition_dissector(parameter_tvb, actx->pinfo, tree, NULL);
        found = TRUE;
        break;
      }
    }
  }
  if (!found) {
    proto_item *ti = proto_tree_add_item(tree, hf_e2ap_ran_function_name_not_recognised, tvb, 0, 0, ENC_NA);
    expert_add_info_format(actx->pinfo, ti, &ei_e2ap_ran_function_names_no_match,
                           "ShortName does not match any known Service Model");
  }
```

The problem here is that the `strcmp(tvb_get_ptr(parameter_tvb, m, name_len), g_ran_functioname_table[n].name)` call contains 2 buffers guaranteed to have two different lengths: the `tvb_get_ptr(parameter_tvb, m, name_len)` pointer will point to a buffer that is `name_len` bytes long, and `g_ran_functioname_table[n].name` will point to a string of length `name_len + 1` (because of the terminating nullbyte). Ultimately this means that if the first `name_len` bytes are the same, we will read past the end of the`tvb_get_ptr` buffer. In the best case, the byte after the buffer happens to be a null byte and `strcmp` detects that the substring which we wanted, but in the worst case (if the data in this buffer has been copied to a new location, or if theres a maliciously crafted packet) then we will read past the end of the buffer into potentially uninitialized memory. Beyond this there is also the danger that strcmp will ignore the substring we are looking for, e.g. if we have the string "fooBARsomething" and the ran function name we are looking for is "BAR" then we would compare "BARs" with "BAR\0".

Hopefully this summarizes the issue and the proposed fix.

To test the changes run `make generate_dissector-e2ap && make -j dissectors` before building wireshark as normal.

Thank you for your time, please let me know if there is anything wrong with the PR, or if the changes could be improved or done in a different way. wireshark is an absolutely awesome piece of software so I'd love to use this opportunity to thank all it's developers!

